  <main class="ins">
    <article class="container ins-article">
      <figure class="ins-art">
        <img src="/assets/insights/we-became-our-parents.jpg" alt="We Became Our Parents — The Talent Noticed. A Street Economics and The Music Cities crossover." width="1600" height="900" fetchpriority="high" />
      </figure>
      <header class="ins-hero">
        <p class="ins-hero__eyebrow">Special Insight</p>
        <h1 class="ins-hero__title">We Became Our Parents</h1>
        <p class="ins-hero__sub">The Talent Noticed</p>
        <p class="ins-hero__dek">A song banned in Japanese kindergartens in 2021, the panel show that tried to litigate it, and why every city is now operating as though it is still 1994. They no longer control the exits.</p>
        <p class="ins-hero__meta">A <strong>Street Economics</strong> &times; <strong>The Music Cities</strong> crossover</p>
      </header>

      <div class="ins-body">
        <p class="ins-lede">A song got banned in Japanese kindergartens in 2021. What the adults in that argument failed to notice is that they no longer control the exits.</p>

        <p>In 2021 a number of Japanese nursery schools and kindergartens banned a pop song. Fuji TV&rsquo;s daytime program Viking MORE convened a segment on the problem. A commentator had to go on television and patiently explain that small children sing catchy things without understanding them, and that there was no practical way to keep them from hearing a song that was playing on television and in shops. The song was <span lang="ja">&#12358;&#12387;&#12379;&#12359;&#12431;</span>, which translates roughly as shut [the f] up, or you are too loud. It was the debut single of an eighteen-year-old who had never shown her face (and still hasn&rsquo;t). See for yourself:</p>

        <ul class="ins-links">
          <li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCln9P4Qm3-EAY4aiEPmRwEA" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ado on YouTube</a> <span class="ins-links__note">9.5m subscribers</span></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qp3b-RXtz4w" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original YouTube video</a> <span class="ins-links__note">429m views</span></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ujyJRxAKpVE&list=RDujyJRxAKpVE&start_radio=1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ado performing it on The First Take</a> <span class="ins-links__note">our preferred version</span></li>
        </ul>

        <div class="ins-video">
          <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/ujyJRxAKpVE" title="Ado — うっせぇわ on THE FIRST TAKE" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen></iframe>
        </div>

        <p>Consider what was actually in the song that required a panel. A young worker describes the unwritten rules of Japanese adult life and finds them intolerable. The etiquette. Pouring the senior&rsquo;s drink at a &lsquo;drinking party&rsquo;. The standing obligation to be agreeable. Removing the chicken from the skewer to make it easier for them to eat. Ado delivers it with a laugh, a growl and a whisper inside a single vocal, and the complaint is specific rather than vague. It is a description of what the arrangement costs the person at the bottom of it.</p>

        <p>And in the middle she hits you with it:</p>

        <blockquote class="ins-pull">&ldquo;If I&rsquo;ll point the muzzle of words at your head and fire&rdquo;</blockquote>

        <p>Nobody on that panel asked whether the arrangement was worth defending. The question on the table was whether children should be permitted to hear it described.</p>

        <p>I want to be careful about who I am indicting here, because it is us. Gen X spent its own adolescence being told that its music was a social hazard. We had the hearings, the warning labels, the local news segments, the parents&rsquo; groups, the school assemblies about what we were listening to and what it would do to us. We knew exactly how it felt to be treated as a discipline problem by adults who had not bothered to listen to the thing they were banning. Then we got old enough to run the institutions, and when the first Gen Z artist said out loud what the arrangement costs, we convened a panel show about it. Same instinct. Same claim to be protecting children. We became the people we had defined ourselves against, and we did it without appearing to notice.</p>

        <p>I see so much of Gen X in Gen Z today, or at least, remnants of what Gen X use to be. And when I talk about it I mostly see Gen X in denial when they comment on that content and not even realizing just how much they became their parents. If Gen Z is Gen X of yesterday, Gen X today is Al Bundy, still reliving high school glory days.</p>

        <p>That would be an ordinary generational irony except for one thing that has changed underneath it, and the change is economic rather than cultural.</p>

        <p>Ado got into music in 2014 through the Niconico app on a Nintendo 3DS. What caught her was the cover singers who performed without showing their faces. She posted her first cover in January 2017, at fourteen, and recorded in a closet in her family home that she soundproofed with material she bought online. She spent roughly six years accumulating reps inside that scene before any label was involved. Virgin Music released <span lang="ja">&#12358;&#12387;&#12379;&#12359;&#12431;</span> on October 23, 2020, eight days after announcing her, the day before she turned eighteen.</p>

        <p>Read that sequence again as infrastructure rather than biography. The skill was built somewhere. There was a convergence point where talent accumulated technique in front of an audience of peers, with a shared practice, a shared platform and a norm that lowered the cost of entry to almost nothing, because you did not have to be presentable to participate. That is the same function Morrisound Recording served in Tampa, but running on a server instead of a console. By the time the market found her, the development had already happened, and it had happened without any institution&rsquo;s permission.</p>

        <p>The results are not marginal. <span lang="ja">&#12358;&#12387;&#12379;&#12359;&#12431;</span> reached number one on the Oricon digital singles and streaming charts in February 2021 and number one on the Billboard Japan Hot 100 in March. It passed one hundred million YouTube views one hundred and forty eight days after release, and one hundred million Billboard Japan streams seventeen weeks after it first charted, the sixth fastest in the chart&rsquo;s history and the youngest solo singer to do it. It finished seventh on the 2021 year end Japan Hot 100 and sits twenty third all time. The artist the kindergartens banned went on to play two nights at Japan National Stadium in April 2024, the first solo female artist to do it, and two nights at Nissan Stadium in July 2026. Her 2025 Hibana tour covered thirty three cities and more than five hundred thousand people, the largest global tour any Japanese artist has run.</p>

        <p>Here is the part that cities need to sit with. At no point in that climb did she require (or ask for) the approval of the adults who objected to her. She did not need a venue circuit to vouch for her, a radio programmer to add her, a school arts program to identify her, or a local scene to adopt her. The closet and the platform were sufficient to build an audience, and the audience was sufficient to make the institutions irrelevant to the outcome the same way that Gen X is irrelevant to Gen Z; Gen X, we are only relevant to ourselves. The institutions got to have an opinion. They did not get a vote.</p>

        <p>This is the condition every city is now operating in, and most are operating as though it is still 1994 (or 1986). When a young person with talent can reach an audience without passing through any local gatekeeper, the local gatekeepers are irrelevant. They are now optional services competing for voluntary participation. A city that treats its young talent and subcultures as a nuisance to be managed is not going to get a slower, milder version of the same result. It is going to get abandoned, and it will not find out until the talent is already somewhere else, because leaving no longer requires anyone to announce it or even to physically move.</p>

        <p>That is what makes the 2021 panel show worth revisiting now rather than filing as a curiosity. The adults in that argument believed they were deciding something. They were not. They were describing themselves to an audience of young people who had already discovered they did not need them, and who were listening carefully.</p>

        <p>Ado has only recently begun loosening the anonymity. Her February 2026 Vivarium video was the first time her face appeared partially unobscured. So this is not a story with a settled ending, which is precisely why it is the right place to start a trip built on finding out how a country produces this much talent and what its cities do with it.</p>

        {(TMC_EP || SETV_EP) ? (
          <section class="ins-watch">
            <h2 class="ins-h2">Watch</h2>
            {TMC_EP && (
